==English==

===Etymology===
An [[allusion]] to a situation in which a hunting dog mistakenly identifies the tree up which it has chased an animal and positions itself at the base of another tree, barking upward at the branches.

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To do, believe, or [[pursue]] something [[wrong]] or [[inappropriate]]; to take the wrong [[approach]]; to follow a false [[lead]]; to [[blame]] or [[rebuke]] the wrong person.
#* {{quote-text|year=1894|author={{w|Robert Barr}}|title=In the Midst of Alarms|chapter=21|passage=You're not the first man who has made such a mistake, and found he was '''barking up the wrong tree'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=1915|author={{w|John Buchan}}|title=The Thirty-Nine Steps|chapter=10|passage=They all went into the house, and left me feeling a precious idiot. I had been '''barking up the wrong tree''' this time.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=1922|author={{w|William MacLeod Raine}}|title=Man Size|chapter=19|passage="We want West. He's a cowardly murderer—killed the man who trusted him." . . .}}
#*: "Of course we may be '''barking up the wrong tree'''," the officer reflected aloud. "Maybe West isn't within five hundred miles of here."
#* '''2008''' September 2, [[w:Ken Russell|Ken Russell]], "[http://entertainment.timesonline.co.uk/tol/arts_and_entertainment/film/article4654486.ece Let my life flash before you, in paperback]," ''Times Online'' (UK) <small>(retrieved 1 Oct 2010)</small>:
#*: After three failed marriages I realised that I may have been '''barking up the wrong tree''' and should abandon the search for the perfect wife.

====Usage notes====
* Often used in the [[present participle|present participial]] form: ''[[barking up the wrong tree]]''.

